,title,link,text,language,family
0,Duan language (Austroasiatic) - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duan_language_(Austroasiatic),"Duan, Doan, or Halang Doan, is a language spoken by more than 4,000 people on either side of the Laotian - Vietnamese border. There are some 2,346 speakers in Attopu Province, Laos, and another couple of thousand in Kon Tum Province, Vietnam.",Halang,Austro-Asiatic
1,Austroasiatic languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Austroasiatic_languages,"The Austroasiatic languages[note 1] (/ ˌɒstroʊ.eɪʒiˈætɪk, ˌɔː -/ ⓘ OSS-troh-ay-zhee-AT-ik, AWSS-) are a large language family spoken throughout Mainland Southeast Asia, South Asia, and East Asia. These languages are natively spoken by the majority of the population in Vietnam and Cambodia, and by minority populations scattered throughout parts of Thailand, Laos, India, Myanmar ...",Halang,Austro-Asiatic
2,AustroAsiatic - Max Planck Society,https://lingweb.eva.mpg.de/channumerals/AustroAsiatic.htm,"Duan: Halang Doan Jeh-Halang: Halang, Jeh Rengao: Rengao Sedang-Todrah: Sedang: Hre, Sedang, Todrah-Monom: Monom, Todrah South Bahnaric: Budeh Stieng (Lower Stieng) Sre-Mnong: Mnong: Eastern Mong: Eastern Mnong Southern-Central Mnong: Kraol, Central Mnong, Southern Mnong Sre: Ko'ho, Maa Stieng-Chrau: Chrau, Mel-Khaonh, Bulo Stieng (Upper Stieng ...",Halang,Austro-Asiatic
3,Duan language (Austroasiatic) — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/duan_language_austroasiatic,"The Duan language, also known as Halang Doan (ISO 639-3: hld), is a threatened Austroasiatic language of the Bahnaric branch, spoken by approximately 5,000 people (as of 2020) primarily along the border between Vietnam and Laos. [1] [2] It belongs to the North Bahnaric subgroup and is classified under the broader Austroasiatic family, which encompasses over 150 languages across Southeast Asia ...",Halang,Austro-Asiatic
